 "result_text": "I changed `get_adapted_type` in `src/sqlacodegen/generators.py` so MySQL `DOUBLE(11,2)` is no longer converted to the generic `Double(11, True)`. I haven't run it against a MySQL database, only the existing test suite, which passes (94 tests).\n\n**Cause:** the code accepts a generic `Float`/`Double` even when its compiled SQL differs from the original column type. That exception was meant for PostgreSQL only, where `Float` can stand in for both `REAL` and `DOUBLE_PRECISION`. It was applied to every dialect, so on MySQL the scale was lost and `asdecimal=True` was rendered as the second argument.\n\n**Fix:** the exception now applies only when the dialect is PostgreSQL. On other dialects the adaptation stops when the compiled type differs, so the dialect-specific type is kept. I'd expect MySQL's `DOUBLE(11, 2)` to be generated as the dialect's own type again. I didn't add a test for it, as you asked for source changes only.",
